// Issue #319 (reviewer round 2): a `return` out of a still-open try/catch
// must not leak the managed backends' handler-liveness counter (CLR:
// LoxOps.HandlerLive; JVM: LoxOps.isHandlerLive() — src/vm.h's own
// VM::m_handlerStack has no such leak, since it is frame-scoped and native
// pops it explicitly on every frame exit, not just a `return` reached via
// its own bytecode-level POP_HANDLER). pre()'s `return 1;` inside its own
// try — no defer, no fault, unrelated to g() below — exits via `leave`/
// `areturn` from inside a still-open protected region, past the point
// each backend's normal-exit cleanup runs. Before the fix, this left the
// counter permanently inflated by one, so g()'s later, unrelated arity
// fault (no `try` live anywhere by the time g() runs) looked catchable
// and ran its pending defer. Delete the `pre();` call and all three
// backends already agreed, proving pre()'s own leaked count — not
// anything about g() itself — is what this probe pins.
fun note(s) { print s; }
fun pre() { try { return 1; } catch (e) { return 2; } }
fun h(a) { return a; }
fun g() { defer note("defer-g"); h(); }
pre();
g();
